	// A[r][s] = B[r][s]
	//
	// Compared to the naive triple loop, we:
	//   * exploit symmetry B[r][s] == B[s][r] and compute only the
	//     lower triangle (r >= s),
	//   * mirror the result to the upper triangle.
	// This halves the number of arithmetic operations while keeping
	// the accumulation order over t for each (r,s) pair unchanged,
	// so we preserve numerical behaviour.

	// B has the same structure as A
